 impossible to prevent non-payers from consuming the good","subtopic":"1.3 Market failure"},{"id":"PUBLIC_GOOD_DEF","label":"Public good: a good that is both non-rival and non-excludable","subtopic":"1.3 Market failure"},{"id":"PRIVATE_GOOD_DEF","label":"Private good: a good that is both rival and excludable","subtopic":"1.3 Market failure"},{"id":"FREE_RIDER","label":"Free rider problem: non-payers can benefit without contributing due to non-excludability","subtopic":"1.3 Market failure"},{"id":"UNDERPROVISION_PG","label":"Under-provision/non-provision of public goods by the private sector","subtopic":"1.3 Market failure"},{"id":"SYM_INFO","label":"Symmetric information: all parties to a transaction have the same/full information","subtopic":"1.3 Market failure"},{"id":"ASYM_INFO","label":"Asymmetric information: one party to a transaction has more/better information than the other","subtopic":"1.3 Market failure"},{"id":"MISALLOCATION_INFO","label":"Imperfect/asymmetric information leading to misallocation of resources","subtopic":"1.3 Market failure"},{"id":"MF","label":"Market failure (recap): free markets misallocate resources","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"QS","label":"Quantitative skills for macroeconomic analysis (e.g. % changes, index numbers)","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"CRIT","label":"Criteria for success of macroeconomic policy (growth, employment, inflation, trade)","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"HIST","label":"Knowledge of the UK economy and policy over the last 10 years","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"DS_D","label":"Draw a demand curve","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"DS_S","label":"Draw a supply curve","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"SHIFT","label":"Shifting supply/demand curves (parallel vs pivotal shifts)","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"TAX_SPEC","label":"Specific (fixed per-unit) indirect tax: definition","subtopic":"1.4 