ucers'/international competitiveness","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"SUB_COST1","label":"Opportunity cost of subsidy spending/taxpayer burden","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"SUB_COST2","label":"Risk of producer inefficiency/subsidy dependency","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"SUB_COST3","label":"Difficulty of government determining correct subsidy size","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"SUB_COST4","label":"Risk subsidy is not passed on to consumers","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"MAXP_DEF","label":"Maximum price: definition (price ceiling set below equilibrium)","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"MAXP_DIAG","label":"Diagram: maximum price below equilibrium causing shortage","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"MAXP_PURPOSE","label":"Reasons for setting a maximum price (protect consumers, affordability)","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"MAXP_BEN1","label":"Maximum price improves affordability/access","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"MAXP_BEN2","label":"Maximum price prevents exploitation by firms with market power","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"MAXP_COST1","label":"Maximum price creates excess demand/shortage","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"MAXP_COST2","label":"Non-price rationing/queuing resulting from shortage","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"MAXP_COST3","label":"Black markets resulting from shortage","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"MAXP_COST4","label":"Producers cut quality to protect margins, undermining affordability gain","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"MAXP_COST5","label":"Reduced long-run investment/supply","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"MINP_DEF","label":"Minimum price: definition (price floor set above equilibrium)","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"MINP_DIAG","label":"Diagram: minimum price above equilibrium causing surplus","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"MINP_PURPOSE","label":"Reasons for setting a minimum price (protect producer/worker income, discourage demerit goods)","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"MINP_BEN1","label":"Minimum price guarantees minimum income for producers/workers","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"MINP_BEN2","label":"Minimum price reduces consumption of demerit goods","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"MINP_COST1","label":"Minimum price creates excess supply/surplus","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"MINP_COST2","label":"Government cost of buying/storing surplus","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"MINP_COST3","label":"Minimum price causes unemployment (labour market case)","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"MINP_COST4","label":"Black markets selling below the minimum price","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"TPP_DEF","label":"Tradable pollution permits: definition (cap-and-trade scheme)","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"TPP_PURPOSE","label":"Reasons for using tradable pollution permits (correct pollution externality)","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"TPP_MECH","label":"Permit trading mechanism creates a market price for pollution","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"TPP_BEN1","label":"Permits give incentive for least-cost/dynamic abatement","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"TPP_BEN2","label":"Cap ensures total pollution is limited","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"TPP_BEN3","label":"Government revenue from auctioning permits","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"TPP_COST1","label":"Difficulty setting the correct cap/permit price","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"TPP_COST2","label":"Administrative/monitoring costs of permit scheme","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"TPP_COST3","label":"International competitiveness issues/carbon leakage","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"PG_NONEX","label":"Public goods: non-excludability","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"PG_NONRIV","label":"Public goods: non-rivalry","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"PG_FREE","label":"Free-rider problem from non-excludability","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"PG_PROVISION","label":"State provision of public goods as a solution to the missing market","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"PG_BEN1","label":"State provision corrects the missing market for public goods","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"PG_BEN2","label":"State provision ensures equitable access regardless of ability to pay","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"PG_COST1","label":"Government lacks a price signal to judge correct provision level","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"PG_COST2","label":"Opportunity cost of taxation funding provision","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"PG_COST3","label":"Risk of inefficiency without profit motive","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"INFO_DEF","label":"Provision of information: definition (government supplies/mandates information)","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"INFO_PURPOSE","label":"Reasons for providing information (correct information gaps)","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"INFO_BEN1","label":"Information provision enables more informed choices, correcting under/over-consumption","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"INFO_BEN2","label":"Information provision is relatively low-cost/non-distortionary","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"INFO_COST1","label":"Information may not change behaviour (habit/addiction)","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"INFO_COST2","label":"Cost of information campaigns","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"INFO_COST3","label":"Risk of information overload/misunderstanding","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"REG_DEF","label":"Regulation: definition (rules/laws controlling behaviour)","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"REG_PURPOSE","label":"Reasons for regulation (directly control behaviour causing market failure)","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"REG_BEN1","label":"Regulation can quickly/effectively stop harmful activity","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"REG_BEN2","label":"Clear rules ease compliance and monitoring","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"REG_COST1","label":"Monitoring and enforcement costs of regulation","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"REG_COST2","label":"Risk of regulatory capture","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"REG_COST3","label":"Regulation gives no incentive to exceed the minimum standard","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"REG_COST4","label":"Firms may relocate to avoid regulation","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"GF_DEF","label":"Government failure: intervention resulting in a net welfare loss","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"GF_CAUSE1","label":"Cause of government failure: distortion of price signals","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"GF_CAUSE2","label":"Cause of government failure: unintended consequences","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"GF_CAUSE3","label":"Cause of government failure: excessive administrative costs","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"GF_CAUSE4","label":"Cause of government failure: information gaps","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"GF_MARKETS","label":"Government failure occurring across various markets","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"EVAL_GI","label":"Evaluating government intervention against policy success criteria and UK evidence","subtopic":"1.4 Government intervention"},{"id":"GDP_DEF","label":"Gross Domestic Product (GDP): total value of output/income/expenditure in an economy","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"REAL_NOMINAL","label":"Distinction between real and nominal values","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"TOTAL_PERCAP","label":"Distinction between total and per capita measures","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"VALUE_VOLUME","label":"Distinction between value and volume","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"RGDP_GROWTH","label":"Rate of change of real GDP as a measure of economic growth","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"GNI","label":"Gross National Income (GNI) as an alternative national income measure","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"PPP_DEF","label":"Purchasing Power Parity (PPP): adjusting exchange rates for cost-of-living differences","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"PPP_ADJUSTED","label":"Use of PPP-adjusted figures in international comparisons","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"GROWTH_COMPARISON","label":"Comparing growth rates between countries and over time","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"GDP_LIM_DISTRIBUTION","label":"GDP limitation: hides income distribution/inequality","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"GDP_LIM_INFORMAL","label":"GDP limitation: excludes informal/non-marketed economic activity","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"GDP_LIM_QUALITY","label":"GDP limitation: fails to capture quality improvements in goods","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"GDP_LIM_EXTERNALITY","label":"GDP limitation: ignores negative externalities/environmental damage","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"GDP_LIM_WELLBEING","label":"GDP limitation: fails to capture broader wellbeing (health, leisure, life expectancy)","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"GDP_LIM_COMPOSITION","label":"GDP limitation: ignores composition of output (e.g. military vs consumer goods)","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"UK_WELLBEING","label":"UK national wellbeing measures","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"HAPPINESS_INCOME","label":"Relationship between real incomes and subjective happiness","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"INFLATION_DEF","label":"Inflation: a sustained rise in the general price level","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"DEFLATION_DEF","label":"Deflation: a sustained fall in the general price level","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"DISINFLATION_DEF","label":"Disinflation: a fall in the rate of inflation","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"CPI_CALC","label":"Calculating the UK rate of inflation using the CPI","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"CPI_LIM_BASKET","label":"CPI limitation: basket may not represent all households","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"CPI_LIM_SUBSTITUTION","label":"CPI limitation: substitution bias from fixed weights","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"CPI_LIM_QUALITY","label":"CPI limitation: fails to fully adjust for quality changes","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"CPI_LIM_HOUSING","label":"CPI limitation: limited treatment of housing costs","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"CPI_LIM_TIMELINESS","label":"CPI limitation: basket/weights based on lagged spending data","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"RPI_DEF","label":"Retail Prices Index (RPI) as an alternative measure of inflation","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"DEMAND_PULL","label":"Demand-pull inflation","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"COST_PUSH","label":"Cost-push inflation","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"MONEY_SUPPLY_GROWTH","label":"Growth of the money supply as a cause of inflation","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"INFLATION_EFFECT_CONSUMERS","label":"Effects of inflation on consumers","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"INFLATION_EFFECT_FIRMS","label":"Effects of inflation on firms","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"INFLATION_EFFECT_GOVERNMENT","label":"Effects of inflation on the government","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"INFLATION_EFFECT_WORKERS","label":"Effects of inflation on workers","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"UNEMPLOYMENT_DEF","label":"Unemployment: people willing and able to work but without a job","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"CLAIMANT_COUNT","label":"Claimant count measure of unemployment","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"ILO_LFS","label":"ILO measure and UK Labour Force Survey","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"UNDEREMPLOYMENT_DIST","label":"Distinction between unemployment and under-employment","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"EMPLOYMENT_RATE_SIG","label":"Significance of changes in the employment rate","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"UNEMPLOYMENT_RATE_SIG","label":"Significance of changes in the unemployment rate","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"INACTIVITY_RATE_SIG","label":"Significance of changes in the inactivity rate","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"STRUCTURAL_UNEMP","label":"Structural unemployment","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"FRICTIONAL_UNEMP","label":"Frictional unemployment","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"SEASONAL_UNEMP","label":"Seasonal unemployment","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"DEMAND_DEFICIENT_UNEMP","label":"Demand-deficient/cyclical unemployment","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"REAL_WAGE_INFLEX","label":"Real wage inflexibility as a cause of unemployment","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"MIGRATION_SKILLS_SIG","label":"Significance of migration and skills for employment and unemployment","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"UNEMP_EFFECT_CONSUMERS","label":"Effects of unemployment on consumers","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"UNEMP_EFFECT_FIRMS","label":"Effects of unemployment on firms","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"UNEMP_EFFECT_WORKERS","label":"Effects of unemployment on workers","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"UNEMP_EFFECT_GOVERNMENT","label":"Effects of unemployment on the government","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"UNEMP_EFFECT_SOCIETY","label":"Effects of unemployment on society","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"BOP_COMPONENTS","label":"Components of the balance of payments","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"CURRENT_ACCOUNT","label":"The current account of the balance of payments","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"TRADE_BALANCE","label":"Balance of trade in goods and services","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"CA_DEFICIT_SURPLUS","label":"Current account deficits and surpluses","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"CA_MACRO_RELATIONSHIP","label":"Relationship between current account imbalances and other macroeconomic objectives","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"INTERCONNECTEDNESS","label":"Interconnectedness of economies through international trade","subtopic":"2.1 Measures of economic performance"},{"id":"AD_COMPONENTS","label":"Components of AD: C + I + G + (X-M)","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"AD_RELATIVE_IMPORTANCE","label":"Relative importance of the components of AD","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"AD_CURVE","label":"The AD curve (price level vs real output)","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"AD_MOVEMENT_SHIFT","label":"Movement along vs shift of the AD curve","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"C_DISPOSABLE_INCOME","label":"Disposable income and its influence on consumer spending","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"C_SAVINGS_RELATIONSHIP","label":"Relationship between savings and consumption","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"C_INTEREST_RATES","label":"Interest rates as an influence on consumer spending","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"C_CONSUMER_CONFIDENCE","label":"Consumer confidence as an influence on consumer spending","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"C_WEALTH_EFFECTS","label":"Wealth effects as an influence on consumer spending","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"I_GROSS_NET","label":"Distinction between gross and net investment","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"I_ECON_GROWTH","label":"Rate of economic growth as an influence on investment","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"I_BUSINESS_CONFIDENCE","label":"Business expectations and confidence as an influence on investment","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"I_ANIMAL_SPIRITS","label":"Keynes and 'animal spirits'","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"I_EXPORT_DEMAND","label":"Demand for exports as an influence on investment","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"I_INTEREST_RATES","label":"Interest rates as an influence on investment","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"I_CREDIT_ACCESS","label":"Access to credit as an influence on investment","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"I_GOVT_REGULATION","label":"Influence of government and regulations on investment","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"G_TRADE_CYCLE","label":"The trade cycle as an influence on government expenditure","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"G_FISCAL_POLICY","label":"Fiscal policy as an influence on government expenditure","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"NT_REAL_INCOME","label":"Real income as an influence on the net trade balance","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"NT_EXCHANGE_RATES","label":"Exchange rates as an influence on the net trade balance","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"NT_WORLD_ECONOMY","label":"State of the world economy as an influence on the net trade balance","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"NT_PROTECTIONISM","label":"Degree of protectionism as an influence on the net trade balance","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"NT_NONPRICE","label":"Non-price factors as an influence on the net trade balance","subtopic":"2.2 Aggregate demand (AD)"},{"id":"AS_DEF","label":"The AS curve: relationship between the price level and total quantity of output firms are willing to supply","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"AS_MOVE_SHIFT","label":"Distinction between a movement along the AS curve and a shift of the AS curve","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"SRAS_DEF","label":"Short-run aggregate supply (SRAS) curve: output supplied when at least one factor cost is fixed","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"LRAS_DEF","label":"Long-run aggregate supply (LRAS) curve: output supplied when all factor costs are flexible, reflecting productive capacity","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"SRAS_LRAS_REL","label":"Relationship between short-run AS and long-run AS (e.g. output gaps as SRAS diverges from LRAS)","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"SRAS_RAWMAT","label":"Factor shifting SRAS: changes in costs of raw materials and energy","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"SRAS_FX","label":"Factor shifting SRAS: changes in exchange rates","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"SRAS_FX_IMPORT","label":"Exchange rate change alters the price of imported raw materials/components","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"SRAS_TAX","label":"Factor shifting SRAS: changes in tax rates affecting firms' costs","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"SRAS_COST_MECH","label":"A change in firms' production costs shifts the SRAS curve","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"SRAS_SHIFT","label":"SRAS curve shifts left (decrease) or right (increase)","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"LRAS_KEYNESIAN","label":"Keynesian shape of the LRAS curve (elastic with spare capacity, becoming inelastic at full capacity)","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"LRAS_CLASSICAL","label":"Classical shape of the LRAS curve (perfectly inelastic at full employment output)","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"LRAS_TECH","label":"Factor shifting LRAS: technological advances","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"LRAS_PROD","label":"Factor shifting LRAS: changes in relative productivity","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"LRAS_EDU","label":"Factor shifting LRAS: changes in education and skills","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"LRAS_REG","label":"Factor shifting LRAS: changes in government regulations","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"LRAS_DEMO","label":"Factor shifting LRAS: demographic changes and migration","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"LRAS_COMP","label":"Factor shifting LRAS: competition policy","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"LRAS_SHIFT","label":"LRAS curve shifts, changing the economy's productive potential","subtopic":"2.3 Aggregate supply (AS)"},{"id":"CF_BASIC","label":"The circular flow of income (households, firms, factor and goods markets)","subtopic":"2.4 National income"},{"id":"INC_WEALTH","label":"Distinction between income (flow) and wealth (stock)","subtopic":"2.4 National income"},{"id":"CF_SECTORS","label":"Injections (investment, government spending, exports) and withdrawals (saving, taxation, imports)","subtopic":"2.4 National income"},{"id":"CF_IMPACT","label":"Impact of injections and withdrawals on the circular flow (expansion/contraction of national income)","subtopic":"2.4 National income"},{"id":"AD_CURVE","label":"Construct an aggregate demand (AD) curve","subtopic":"2.4 National income"},{"id":"AS_CURVE","label":"Construct an aggregate supply (AS) curve","subtopic":"2.4 National income"},{"id":"EQ_CONCEPT","label":"The concept of equilibrium real national output","subtopic":"2.4 National income"},{"id":"EQ_DIAGRAM","label":"Using AD/AS diagrams to show shifts causing changes in equilibrium price level and real national output","subtopic":"2.4 National income"},{"id":"MPC","label":"Marginal propensity to consume (MPC)","subtopic":"2.4 National income"},{"id":"MPS","label":"Marginal propensity to save (MPS)","subtopic":"2.4 National income"},{"id":"MPT","label":"Marginal propensity to tax (MPT)","subtopic":"2.4 National income"},{"id":"MPM","label":"Marginal propensity to import (MPM)","subtopic":"2.4 National income"},{"id":"MPW","label":"Marginal propensity to withdraw (MPW = MPS + MPT + MPM)","subtopic":"2.4 National income"},{"id":"MULT_PROCESS","label":"The multiplier process (rounds of respending of an initial injection)","subtopic":"2.4 National income"},{"id":"MULT_RATIO","label":"The multiplier ratio (change in national income relative to initial change in injection)","subtopic":"2.4 National income"},{"id":"MULT_CALC","label":"Calculating the multiplier using 1/(1-MPC) and 1/MPW","subtopic":"2.4 National income"},{"id":"MULT_EFFECTS","label":"Effects of the multiplier on the economy (output, employment, price level)","subtopic":"2.4 National income"},{"id":"MULT_AD_SIG","label":"Significance of the multiplier for the size of shifts in AD","subtopic":"2.4 National income"},{"id":"GROWTH_DEF","label":"Economic growth: increase in real output (real GDP) over time","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"ACTUAL_GROWTH","label":"Actual growth: percentage increase in real GDP, driven by AD","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"POTENTIAL_GROWTH","label":"Potential growth: increase in the productive capacity of the economy","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"DIST_ACTUAL_POTENTIAL","label":"Distinction between actual and potential growth","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"CONSUMPTION_FACTOR","label":"Rising consumption increases AD, causing actual growth","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"INVESTMENT_FACTOR","label":"Rising investment increases AD (actual growth) and capital stock (potential growth)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"GOV_SPEND_FACTOR","label":"Rising government spending increases AD, causing actual growth","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"NET_EXPORTS_FACTOR","label":"Rising net exports increase AD, causing actual growth","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"FACTOR_QUANTITY","label":"Increase in quantity of factors of production raises potential output","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"FACTOR_QUALITY","label":"Increase in quality/productivity of factors of production raises potential output","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"TECH_PROGRESS","label":"Technological progress/innovation raises potential output","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"TRADE_SPECIALISATION","label":"International trade allows specialisation by comparative advantage, raising output/efficiency","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"TRADE_ECONOMIES_SCALE","label":"Access to larger overseas markets allows firms to exploit economies of scale","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"TRADE_COMPETITION","label":"Exposure to international competition drives innovation and productivity gains","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"TREND_GROWTH_DEF","label":"Long-term trend growth rate: average sustainable rate of growth of potential output","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"DIST_ACTUAL_TREND","label":"Distinction between actual growth rates and long-term trend growth rate","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"OUTPUT_GAP_DEF","label":"Output gap: difference between actual output and potential output","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"POS_OUTPUT_GAP","label":"Positive output gap: actual output exceeds potential output (overheating)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"NEG_OUTPUT_GAP","label":"Negative output gap: actual output below potential output (spare capacity)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"OUTPUT_GAP_MEASURE_DIFF","label":"Difficulties in measuring output gaps (potential output unobservable, estimates revised)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"DRAW_AD","label":"Construct/draw an aggregate demand (AD) curve","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"DRAW_SRAS","label":"Construct/draw a short-run aggregate supply (SRAS) curve","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"DRAW_LRAS","label":"Construct/draw a long-run aggregate supply (LRAS)/potential output line","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"OUTPUT_GAP_DIAGRAM","label":"Illustrating a positive/negative output gap on an AD/AS diagram","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"BUSINESS_CYCLE_DEF","label":"The trade (business) cycle: fluctuations of actual growth around the long-term trend","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"BOOM_DEF","label":"A boom: phase where actual growth exceeds trend, economy beyond sustainable capacity","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"BOOM_INFLATION","label":"Rising demand-pull inflation during a boom","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"BOOM_UNEMPLOYMENT","label":"Falling unemployment during a boom","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"BOOM_CONFIDENCE","label":"High business/consumer confidence and investment during a boom","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"BOOM_IMPORTS","label":"Rising imports/worsening trade balance during a boom","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"RECESSION_DEF","label":"A recession: two consecutive quarters of negative economic growth","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"RECESSION_UNEMPLOYMENT","label":"Rising unemployment during a recession","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"RECESSION_INFLATION","label":"Falling inflation/deflationary pressure during a recession","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"RECESSION_CONFIDENCE","label":"Falling business/consumer confidence, investment and spending during a recession","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"CONS_BENEFIT_INCOME","label":"Consumers: growth raises incomes/employment, increasing consumption possibilities (benefit)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"CONS_BENEFIT_CHOICE","label":"Consumers: growth increases variety/quality of goods available (benefit)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"CONS_COST_INFLATION","label":"Consumers: growth-driven inflation erodes real purchasing power (cost)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"CONS_COST_INEQUALITY","label":"Consumers: benefits of growth may be unevenly distributed, widening inequality (cost)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"FIRM_BENEFIT_PROFIT","label":"Firms: growth raises demand/sales, boosting revenue and profits (benefit)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"FIRM_BENEFIT_INVESTMENT","label":"Firms: higher profits/confidence encourage further investment and economies of scale (benefit)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"FIRM_COST_CAPACITY","label":"Firms: growth raises costs (wages, materials) and requires costly capacity expansion (cost)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"FIRM_COST_COMPETITION","label":"Firms: growth attracts new entrants/competition, eroding market share (cost)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"GOV_BENEFIT_TAXREV","label":"Government: higher incomes/spending raise tax revenue (benefit)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"GOV_BENEFIT_SPENDING","label":"Government: lower unemployment reduces welfare spending, improving public finances (benefit)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"GOV_COST_INFLATION_POLICY","label":"Government: inflationary pressure requires tighter fiscal/monetary policy (cost)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"GOV_COST_CURRENTACCOUNT","label":"Government: growth increases imports, worsening the current account (cost)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"GOV_COST_EXTERNALITY","label":"Government: growth increases negative externalities, requiring extra spending on mitigation (cost)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"CURR_LIVING_BENEFIT","label":"Current living standards improve via greater consumption of goods/services (benefit)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"CURR_LIVING_COST","label":"Current living standards may fall due to externalities and inequality (cost)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"FUTURE_LIVING_BENEFIT","label":"Future living standards: investment-driven potential growth expands future productive capacity (benefit)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"FUTURE_LIVING_COST","label":"Future living standards: resource depletion/environmental damage may undermine sustainability (cost)","subtopic":"2.5 Economic growth"},{"id":"OBJ_GROWTH","label":"Economic growth as a macroeconomic objective","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"OBJ_UNEMP","label":"Low unemployment as a macroeconomic objective","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"OBJ_INFL_LOW","label":"Low inflation as a macroeconomic objective","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"OBJ_INFL_STABLE","label":"Stable inflation as a macroeconomic objective","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"OBJ_BOP","label":"Balance of payments equilibrium on current account","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"OBJ_BUDGET","label":"Balanced government budget objective","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"OBJ_ENV","label":"Protection of the environment objective","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"OBJ_EQUALITY","label":"Greater income equality objective","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"DIST_MON_FISC","label":"Distinction between monetary and fiscal policy","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"BOE_ROLE","label":"Broader role of the Bank of England (Bank Rate, QE, financial stability, lender of last resort)","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"MPC_ROLE","label":"Role and operation of the Monetary Policy Committee","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"MON_INSTR_RATE","label":"Interest rates as a monetary policy instrument","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"MON_INSTR_QE","label":"Quantitative easing (asset purchases) as a monetary policy instrument","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"FISC_INSTR","label":"Government spending and taxation as fiscal policy instruments","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"FISC_DEFICIT_SURPLUS","label":"Distinction between government fiscal deficit and surplus","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"TAX_DIRECT_INDIRECT","label":"Distinction between direct and indirect taxation, with examples","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"DRAW_AD","label":"Construct/draw an AD curve","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"DRAW_AS","label":"Construct/draw an AS curve","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"ADAS_DEMAND_SIDE","label":"Using AD/AS diagrams to illustrate demand-side policy effects","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"HIST_GD","label":"Demand-side policy in the Great Depression: interpretations and US/UK responses","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"HIST_GFC","label":"Demand-side policy in the 2008 Global Financial Crisis: interpretations and US/UK responses","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"ADV_MON_1","label":"Monetary policy is flexible and quickly adjustable","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"ADV_MON_2","label":"Monetary policy benefits from independence from political interference","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"ADV_MON_3","label":"QE can address liquidity shortages/credit crunch at the zero lower bound","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"DIS_MON_1","label":"Monetary policy suffers time lags in transmission","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"DIS_MON_2","label":"Monetary policy has limited effectiveness at the zero lower bound","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"DIS_MON_3","label":"QE risks asset price bubbles and worsening inequality","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"ADV_FISC_1","label":"Fiscal policy can directly target specific sectors/regions","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"ADV_FISC_2","label":"Fiscal policy remains effective when monetary policy is stuck at the zero lower bound","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"DIS_FISC_1","label":"Fiscal policy suffers time lags (recognition, decision, implementation)","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"DIS_FISC_2","label":"Fiscal expansion can cause crowding out of private investment","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"DIS_FISC_3","label":"Fiscal expansion increases government debt/deficit","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"SS_MARKET_BASED","label":"Market-based supply-side policies (definition)","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"SS_INTERVENTIONIST","label":"Interventionist supply-side policies (definition)","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"SS_REASONS","label":"Reasons for pursuing supply-side policy","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"SS_INCENTIVES","label":"Supply-side policies to increase incentives","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"SS_COMPETITION","label":"Supply-side policies to promote competition","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"SS_LABOUR_REFORM","label":"Supply-side policies to reform the labour market","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"SS_SKILLS","label":"Supply-side policies to improve skills/quality of the labour force","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"SS_INFRASTRUCTURE","label":"Supply-side policies to improve infrastructure","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"ADAS_SUPPLY_SIDE","label":"Using AD/AS diagrams to illustrate supply-side policy effects","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"SS_ADV_1","label":"Supply-side policy increases LRAS without causing inflation","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"SS_ADV_2","label":"Supply-side policy improves international competitiveness","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"SS_ADV_3","label":"Market-based policies are cost-effective, relying on the price mechanism","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"SS_ADV_4","label":"Interventionist policies directly correct market failure","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"SS_DIS_1","label":"Supply-side policies take a long time to affect AS (time lags)","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"SS_DIS_2","label":"Supply-side policies are costly, with opportunity cost/impact on deficit","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"SS_DIS_3","label":"Market-based labour reforms may worsen income inequality","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"SS_DIS_4","label":"Supply-side policy is ineffective if the real problem is deficient demand","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"CONF_GROWTH_INFL","label":"Conflict: economic growth vs low inflation","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"CONF_GROWTH_ENV","label":"Conflict: economic growth vs protecting the environment","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"CONF_GROWTH_BOP","label":"Conflict: economic growth vs balance of payments equilibrium","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"CONF_UNEMP_INFL","label":"Conflict: low unemployment vs low inflation","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"CONF_EQUALITY_INCENTIVES","label":"Conflict: income equality vs incentives/growth","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"PHILLIPS_SR","label":"Short-run Phillips curve","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"POLICY_CONFLICTS","label":"Potential policy conflicts and trade-offs","subtopic":"2.6 Macroeconomic objectives and policies"},{"id":"DISECON_DEF","label":"Diseconomies of scale: definition (rising average costs as a firm grows beyond its optimal size)","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"ECON_SCALE_DEF","label":"Economies of scale: definition (falling average costs as output increases)","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"C_MARKET_SIZE","label":"Constraint/reason: size of the market limits potential growth","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"C_FINANCE","label":"Constraint/reason: limited access to finance restricts growth","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"C_OWNER_OBJ","label":"Constraint/reason: owner objectives limit or shape growth ambitions","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"C_REGULATION","label":"Constraint/reason: government regulation restricts business growth","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"SMALL_DISECON","label":"Reason for staying small: risk of diseconomies of scale deters expansion","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"SMALL_NICHE_PRODUCT","label":"Reason for staying small: nature of product/service suits small-scale, personalised production","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"PUB_PRIV_DEF","label":"Distinction between public and private sector organisations","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"PROFIT_NFP_DEF","label":"Distinction between profit and not-for-profit organisations","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"PAP_SEPARATION","label":"Divorce of ownership from control","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"PAP_PROBLEM","label":"The principal-agent problem","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"PAP_SIGNIFICANCE","label":"Significance of the principal-agent problem (agency costs, managerial objectives)","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"G_PROFIT","label":"Growth motive: increase profit/sales revenue","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"G_ECON_SCALE","label":"Growth motive: achieve economies of scale","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"G_MARKET_POWER","label":"Growth motive: increase market power/reduce competition","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"G_RISK","label":"Growth motive: spread risk through diversification","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"G_MANAGERIAL","label":"Growth motive: managerial (agent) self-interest","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"G_SURVIVAL","label":"Growth motive: survival/defensive growth","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"ORGANIC","label":"Organic growth: definition","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"VERT_INT_DEF","label":"Vertical integration: definition","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"VERT_FORWARD","label":"Forward vertical integration: definition","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"VERT_BACKWARD","label":"Backward vertical integration: definition","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"HORIZ_INT","label":"Horizontal integration: definition","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"CONGLOM_INT","label":"Conglomerate integration: definition","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"OA1","label":"Advantage of organic growth: lower risk, controlled pace","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"OA2","label":"Advantage of organic growth: retains culture and management control","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"OA3","label":"Advantage of organic growth: financed internally, avoids external debt/dilution","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"OD1","label":"Disadvantage of organic growth: slower rate of growth","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"OD2","label":"Disadvantage of organic growth: limited by size of existing market","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"OD3","label":"Disadvantage of organic growth: still subject to diseconomies of scale over time","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"VA1","label":"Advantage of vertical integration: control over input supply/quality (backward)","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"VA2","label":"Advantage of vertical integration: control over distribution/market access (forward)","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"VA3","label":"Advantage of vertical integration: eliminates suppliers'/distributors' profit margins","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"VA4","label":"Advantage of vertical integration: creates barriers to entry","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"VD1","label":"Disadvantage of vertical integration: requires different management expertise","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"VD2","label":"Disadvantage of vertical integration: large capital investment required","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"VD3","label":"Disadvantage of vertical integration: complacency in acquired firm reduces efficiency","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"VD4","label":"Disadvantage of vertical integration: regulatory/competition scrutiny risk","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"HA1","label":"Advantage of horizontal integration: rapid increase in market share/power","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"HA2","label":"Advantage of horizontal integration: exploits economies of scale","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"HA3","label":"Advantage of horizontal integration: reduces competition, potential higher prices","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"HA4","label":"Advantage of horizontal integration: rationalisation cuts duplicated costs","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"HD1","label":"Disadvantage of horizontal integration: risk of diseconomies of scale","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"HD2","label":"Disadvantage of horizontal integration: regulatory/competition authority risk","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"HD3","label":"Disadvantage of horizontal integration: cultural clashes reduce efficiency","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"HD4","label":"Disadvantage of horizontal integration: reduced consumer choice","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"CA1","label":"Advantage of conglomerate integration: diversification spreads risk","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"CA2","label":"Advantage of conglomerate integration: access to new markets/customers","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"CA3","label":"Advantage of conglomerate integration: economies of scope from shared resources","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"CD1","label":"Disadvantage of conglomerate integration: lack of managerial expertise in new markets","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"CD2","label":"Disadvantage of conglomerate integration: loss of strategic focus","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"CD3","label":"Disadvantage of conglomerate integration: diversification may serve managers more than shareholders","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"DEMERGER_DEF","label":"Demerger: definition","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"DM_R1","label":"Reason for demerger: refocus on core business","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"DM_R2","label":"Reason for demerger: raise cash by selling non-core divisions","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"DM_R3","label":"Reason for demerger: regulatory-forced divestment","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"DM_R4","label":"Reason for demerger: reverse a failed merger/integration","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"DM_R5","label":"Reason for demerger: unlock shareholder value (conglomerate discount)","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"DM_BUS_FOCUS","label":"Impact of demerger on businesses: improved strategic focus/efficiency","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"DM_BUS_COST","label":"Impact of demerger on businesses: loss of economies of scale/scope raises costs","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"DM_WORKERS_JOBLOSS","label":"Impact of demerger on workers: redundancies/job losses","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"DM_WORKERS_OPPORTUNITY","label":"Impact of demerger on workers: new opportunities/clearer career paths","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"DM_CONSUMERS_CHOICE","label":"Impact of demerger on consumers: increased competition/choice, potentially lower prices","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"DM_CONSUMERS_COST","label":"Impact of demerger on consumers: loss of economies of scale may raise prices","subtopic":"3.1 Business growth"},{"id":"TR_DEF","label":"Total revenue: TR = price × quantity sold","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"TC_DEF","label":"Total cost: sum of fixed and variable costs","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"AR_DEF","label":"Average revenue: AR = TR/Q (= price, the demand curve)","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"AC_DEF","label":"Average cost: AC = TC/Q","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"MR_DEF","label":"Marginal revenue: change in TR from selling one extra unit","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"MC_DEF","label":"Marginal cost: change in TC from producing one extra unit","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"PROFIT_DEF","label":"Profit: Profit = TR − TC","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"DRAW_MC","label":"Drawing a marginal cost (MC) curve","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"DRAW_MR","label":"Drawing a marginal revenue (MR) curve","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"DRAW_AR","label":"Drawing an average revenue/demand (AR) curve","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"DRAW_AC","label":"Drawing an average cost (AC) curve","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"AGENCY_PROBLEM","label":"Divorce of ownership and control / principal-agent problem","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"BOUNDED_RATIONALITY","label":"Bounded rationality and imperfect information limiting managerial decision-making","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"PMAX_REASON","label":"Reasons for pursuing profit maximisation (owner/shareholder wealth, funding growth, survival)","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"PMAX_COND","label":"Profit maximisation condition: output where MC = MR","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"PMAX_DIAGRAM","label":"Diagram illustrating profit maximisation (MC=MR, supernormal profit area)","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"RMAX_REASON","label":"Reasons for pursuing revenue maximisation (managerial pay/status linked to sales/turnover)","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"RMAX_COND","label":"Revenue maximisation condition: output where MR = 0","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"RMAX_DIAGRAM","label":"Diagram illustrating revenue maximisation (MR=0 on AR/MR diagram)","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"SMAX_REASON","label":"Reasons for pursuing sales maximisation (market share, growth, job security, entry deterrence)","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"SMAX_COND","label":"Sales maximisation condition: output where AR = AC (normal profit only)","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"SMAX_DIAGRAM","label":"Diagram illustrating sales maximisation (AR=AC breakeven output)","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"SATIS_DEF","label":"Satisficing: aiming for satisfactory, not maximum, performance across objectives","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"PMAX_ADV_SURVIVAL","label":"Advantage: profit max secures funds for reinvestment, growth and survival","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"PMAX_ADV_SHAREHOLDER","label":"Advantage: profit max rewards shareholders and attracts investment","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"PMAX_ADV_BENCHMARK","label":"Advantage: profit max gives a clear, measurable decision-making benchmark","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"PMAX_DIS_STAKEHOLDER","label":"Disadvantage: profit max ignores interests of other stakeholders (workers, environment, community)","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"PMAX_DIS_SHORTTERM","label":"Disadvantage: profit max can encourage short-termism/cost- and quality-cutting","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"PMAX_DIS_INFO","label":"Disadvantage: identifying the true MC=MR point is difficult due to imperfect information","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"PMAX_DIS_AGENCY","label":"Disadvantage: managers (agents) may not actually pursue profit max due to the agency problem","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"RMAX_ADV_MARKETSHARE","label":"Advantage: revenue max builds market share and brand presence","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"RMAX_ADV_ECONOMIES","label":"Advantage: higher output from revenue max can enable economies of scale","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"RMAX_DIS_PROFIT","label":"Disadvantage: revenue max output yields lower profit than the profit-maximising output","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"RMAX_DIS_SHAREHOLDER","label":"Disadvantage: revenue max can conflict with shareholders' preference for profit","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"SMAX_ADV_MARKETPOWER","label":"Advantage: sales max builds market dominance and barriers to entry","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"SMAX_ADV_ECONOMIES","label":"Advantage: sales max output can secure economies of scale","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"SMAX_DIS_NOSURPLUS","label":"Disadvantage: sales max earns only normal profit, no surplus for investment/dividends","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"SMAX_DIS_RISK","label":"Disadvantage: risk of losses if costs are underestimated beyond the AR=AC output","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"SATIS_ADV_STAKEHOLDER","label":"Advantage: satisficing balances the interests of multiple stakeholders","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"SATIS_ADV_SUSTAINABLE","label":"Advantage: satisficing reduces risk-taking and supports sustainable long-term decisions","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"SATIS_DIS_EFFICIENCY","label":"Disadvantage: satisficing can cause complacency and missed profit opportunities","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"SATIS_DIS_MEASURE","label":"Disadvantage: the 'satisfactory' level is subjective and hard to define/measure","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"SATIS_DIS_SHAREHOLDER","label":"Disadvantage: satisficing may leave shareholders dissatisfied with sub-maximised profit","subtopic":"3.2 Business objectives"},{"id":"REV_TR","label":"Total revenue (TR = P × Q)","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"REV_AR","label":"Average revenue (AR = TR/Q = P)","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"REV_MR","label":"Marginal revenue (MR = ΔTR/ΔQ)","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"REV_REL","label":"Relationship between TR, AR and MR curves","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"PED_CONCEPT","label":"Price elasticity of demand (concept/calculation)","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"PED_REV_REL","label":"Relationship between PED and revenue (TR changes with price)","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"COST_TFC","label":"Total fixed cost","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"COST_TVC","label":"Total variable cost","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"COST_TC","label":"Total cost (TFC + TVC)","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"COST_AFC","label":"Average fixed cost","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"COST_AVC","label":"Average variable cost","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"COST_ATC","label":"Average total cost","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"COST_MC","label":"Marginal cost","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"COST_REL","label":"Relationship between cost curves (TC/AFC/AVC/ATC/MC)","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"DMR_LAW","label":"Law of diminishing marginal returns","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"MP_MC_INVERSE","label":"Inverse relationship between marginal product and marginal cost","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"SR_CURVE_SHAPE","label":"Derivation/shape of short-run cost curves","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"LRAC_ENVELOPE","label":"Long-run average cost curve as envelope of SRAC curves","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"EOS_DEF","label":"Economies of scale (definition)","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"DOS_DEF","label":"Diseconomies of scale (definition)","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"LRAC_SHAPE","label":"U-shaped LRAC from economies then diseconomies of scale","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"MES","label":"Minimum efficient scale","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"IEOS_CAT","label":"Internal economies of scale (concept)","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"IEOS_PURCHASING","label":"Purchasing/bulk-buying economies","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"IEOS_TECHNICAL","label":"Technical economies","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"IEOS_FINANCIAL","label":"Financial economies","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"IEOS_MANAGERIAL","label":"Managerial economies","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"IEOS_MARKETING","label":"Marketing economies","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"IEOS_RISKBEARING","label":"Risk-bearing economies","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"IDOS_CAT","label":"Internal diseconomies of scale (concept)","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"IDOS_COMM","label":"Communication/coordination problems","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"IDOS_MOTIVATION","label":"Worker alienation/motivation problems","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"EEOS_CAT","label":"External economies of scale (concept)","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"EEOS_LABOUR","label":"Access to a skilled labour pool","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"EEOS_INFRASTRUCTURE","label":"Improved local infrastructure","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"EEOS_ANCILLARY","label":"Growth of ancillary/support industries","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"EEOS_KNOWLEDGE","label":"Knowledge sharing/spillovers","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"EDOS_CAT","label":"External diseconomies of scale (concept)","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"EDOS_INPUTCOSTS","label":"Rising input costs from industry-wide competition for resources","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"EDOS_CONGESTION","label":"Congestion/infrastructure strain","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"EDOS_ENV","label":"Environmental costs from industry growth","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"INT_EXT_DIST","label":"Distinction between internal and external economies of scale","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"NORMAL_PROFIT","label":"Normal profit (AR = ATC)","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"SUPERNORMAL_PROFIT","label":"Supernormal profit (AR > ATC)","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"LOSS_DEF","label":"Losses (AR < ATC)","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"MC_MR_RULE","label":"Profit maximisation condition (MC = MR)","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"SR_SHUTDOWN","label":"Short-run shut-down point (AR < AVC)","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"LR_SHUTDOWN","label":"Long-run shut-down point (AR < ATC)","subtopic":"3.3 Revenues, costs and profits"},{"id":"R_MC","label":"Marginal cost (MC) curve","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"R_AC","label":"Average cost (AC) curve","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"R_AR","label":"Average revenue (AR) = firm's demand curve","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"R_MR","label":"Marginal revenue (MR) curve","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"R_PROFITMAX","label":"Profit-maximising rule (MC = MR)","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"R_NORMPROFIT","label":"Normal profit (AR = AC)","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"R_SUPERNORMPROFIT","label":"Supernormal profit (AR > AC)","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"R_BARRIERS","label":"Barriers to entry and exit (general concept)","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"ALLOC_EFF","label":"Allocative efficiency (P = MC)","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"PROD_EFF","label":"Productive efficiency (output at minimum AC)","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"DYN_EFF","label":"Dynamic efficiency","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"X_INEFF","label":"X-inefficiency","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"PC_CHAR","label":"Characteristics of perfect competition","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"PC_SR_EQ","label":"Short-run profit-maximising equilibrium in perfect competition","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"PC_ENTRY_EXIT","label":"Entry/exit mechanism erodes supernormal profit/losses in PC","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"PC_LR_EQ","label":"Long-run equilibrium in perfect competition (normal profit)","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"PC_DIAGRAM","label":"Diagrammatic analysis of perfect competition","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"MC2_CHAR","label":"Characteristics of monopolistically competitive markets","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"MC2_SR_EQ","label":"Short-run profit-maximising equilibrium in monopolistic competition","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"MC2_ENTRY_EXIT","label":"Entry/exit mechanism erodes supernormal profit in monopolistic competition","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"MC2_LR_EQ","label":"Long-run equilibrium in monopolistic competition (tangency, normal profit)","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"MC2_DIAGRAM","label":"Diagrammatic analysis of monopolistic competition","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"OLI_BARRIERS","label":"High barriers to entry and exit (oligopoly characteristic)","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"OLI_CONCRATIO","label":"High concentration ratio (oligopoly characteristic)","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"OLI_INTERDEP","label":"Interdependence of firms (oligopoly characteristic)","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"OLI_PRODDIFF","label":"Product differentiation (oligopoly characteristic)","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"OLI_NFIRM_CALC","label":"Calculation of n-firm concentration ratios","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"OLI_NFIRM_SIGNIF","label":"Significance of concentration ratios","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"GT_PAYOFF","label":"Payoff matrix","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"GT_DOMSTRAT","label":"Dominant strategy","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"GT_NASH","label":"Nash equilibrium","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"GT_PD","label":"Prisoner's dilemma (two-firm/two-outcome model)","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"OLI_COLLUDE_REASONS","label":"Reasons for collusive behaviour","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"OLI_NONCOLLUDE_REASONS","label":"Reasons for non-collusive behaviour","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"OLI_OVERT","label":"Overt collusion and cartels","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"OLI_TACIT","label":"Tacit collusion and price leadership","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"OLI_PRICEWARS","label":"Price wars","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"OLI_PREDATORY","label":"Predatory pricing","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"OLI_LIMITPRICING","label":"Limit pricing","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"OLI_NONPRICE","label":"Types of non-price competition","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"MON_CHAR","label":"Characteristics of monopoly","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"MON_EQ","label":"Profit-maximising equilibrium of monopoly","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"MON_DIAGRAM","label":"Diagrammatic analysis of monopoly","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"PD_CONDITIONS","label":"Necessary conditions for third-degree price discrimination","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"PD_DIAGRAM","label":"Diagrammatic analysis of price discrimination","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"PD_BEN_PRODUCER","label":"Benefit of price discrimination: increased producer revenue/profit","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"PD_BEN_CONSUMER","label":"Benefit of price discrimination: lower price for elastic-segment consumers","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"PD_COST_CONSUMER","label":"Cost of price discrimination: higher price for inelastic-segment consumers","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"PD_COST_PRODUCER","label":"Cost of price discrimination: administrative cost/risk of arbitrage","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"NAT_MONOPOLY","label":"Natural monopoly","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"MON_FIRM_BENEFIT","label":"Benefits of monopoly to firms","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"MON_CONSUMER_BENEFIT","label":"Benefits of monopoly to consumers","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"MON_CONSUMER_COST","label":"Costs of monopoly to consumers","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"MON_EMPLOYEE_IMPACT","label":"Effects of monopoly on employees","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"MON_SUPPLIER_IMPACT","label":"Effects of monopoly on suppliers","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"EFF_PC","label":"Efficiency in perfect competition","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"EFF_MC2","label":"Efficiency in monopolistic competition","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"EFF_OLIGO","label":"Efficiency in oligopoly","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"EFF_MONOPOLY","label":"Efficiency in monopoly","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"MPSY_CHAR","label":"Characteristics and conditions for monopsony","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"MPSY_FIRM_BENEFIT","label":"Benefit of monopsony to firms (lower input costs)","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"MPSY_CONSUMER_BENEFIT","label":"Benefit of monopsony to consumers (potentially lower prices)","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"MPSY_EMPLOYEE_COST","label":"Cost of monopsony to employees (lower wages/exploitation)","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"MPSY_EMPLOYEE_BENEFIT","label":"Benefit of monopsony to employees (job security from stable large employer)","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"MPSY_SUPPLIER_COST","label":"Cost of monopsony to suppliers (lower prices paid)","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"CONT_CHAR","label":"Characteristics of contestable markets","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"CONT_IMPLICATIONS","label":"Implications of contestable markets for firm behaviour","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"CONT_BARRIER_TYPES","label":"Types of barriers to entry and exit","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"CONT_SUNKCOSTS","label":"Sunk costs and the degree of contestability","subtopic":"3.4 Market structures"},{"id":"LAB_DERIVED","label":"Demand for labour as a derived demand","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"MPP","label":"Marginal physical product of labour (diminishing returns)","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"PRICE_OUTPUT","label":"Price of the firm's output/product","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"MRP_DEF","label":"Marginal revenue product theory (MRP = MPP × price of output)","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"FACTOR_SUBSTITUTES","label":"Price of substitute/complementary factors of production (capital)","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"FACTOR_NUMBUYERS","label":"Number of firms/employers demanding labour","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"DRAW_LAB_DEMAND","label":"Construction of the labour demand curve","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"SUP_WAGE","label":"Relative wage rate offered in the occupation","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"SUP_NONMON","label":"Non-monetary benefits/net advantages of the occupation","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"SUP_BARRIERS","label":"Barriers to entry: qualifications and training required","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"SUP_POPULATION","label":"Size of qualified/working population and net migration","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"SUP_UNIONS","label":"Trade union/professional body restrictions on entry","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"DRAW_LAB_SUPPLY","label":"Construction of the labour supply curve","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"COMPETITIVE_LM","label":"Definition of a competitive labour market","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"LAB_EQUILIBRIUM","label":"Diagrammatic labour market equilibrium (wage & employment)","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"GEO_MOBILITY","label":"Definition of geographical mobility of labour","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"GEO_CAUSE_HOUSING","label":"Regional house price/relocation cost differences","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"GEO_CAUSE_FAMILY","label":"Family and social ties limiting relocation","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"GEO_CAUSE_INFO","label":"Imperfect information about jobs elsewhere","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"GEO_IMMOBILITY","label":"Geographical immobility of labour","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"OCC_MOBILITY","label":"Definition of occupational mobility of labour","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"OCC_CAUSE_SKILLS","label":"Lack of transferable skills/qualifications","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"OCC_CAUSE_TRAINING","label":"Time and cost of retraining","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"OCC_CAUSE_DISCRIM","label":"Labour market discrimination restricting occupational mobility","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"OCC_IMMOBILITY","label":"Occupational immobility of labour","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"LAB_MARKET_FAILURE","label":"Market failure caused by labour immobility","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"MONOPSONY_DEF","label":"Definition of a monopsony employer","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"MONOPSONY_DIAGRAM","label":"Diagrammatic analysis of monopsony wage/employment","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"UNION_DEF","label":"Definition of a trade union as monopoly seller of labour","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"UNION_DIAGRAM","label":"Diagrammatic analysis of trade union wage bargaining","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"BILATERAL_MONOPOLY","label":"Bilateral monopoly in the labour market","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"CURRENT_ISSUES","label":"Understanding of current labour market issues","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"PED_LABOUR","label":"Elasticity of demand for labour: definition and determinants","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"PES_LABOUR","label":"Elasticity of supply of labour: definition and determinants","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"ELASTICITY_SIGNIFICANCE","label":"Significance of labour demand/supply elasticities for wage & employment effects","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"MAXWAGE_DEF","label":"Definition of a maximum wage","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"MAXWAGE_ADV","label":"Advantages of a maximum wage","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"MAXWAGE_DIS","label":"Disadvantages of a maximum wage","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"MAXWAGE_EVAL","label":"Evaluation of maximum wage policy","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"MINWAGE_DEF","label":"Definition of a minimum wage","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"MINWAGE_ADV","label":"Advantages of a minimum wage","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"MINWAGE_DIS","label":"Disadvantages of a minimum wage","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"MINWAGE_EVAL","label":"Evaluation of minimum wage policy","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"PUBSEC_DEF","label":"Definition of public sector wage setting","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"PUBSEC_ADV","label":"Advantages of centralised public sector wage setting","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"PUBSEC_DIS","label":"Disadvantages of centralised public sector wage setting","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"PUBSEC_EVAL","label":"Evaluation of public sector wage setting","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"REASONS_IMMOBILITY_POLICY","label":"Reasons for government policy to tackle labour immobility","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"GEO_POLICY","label":"Policies to tackle geographical immobility","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"GEO_POLICY_ADV","label":"Advantages of geographical immobility policies","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"GEO_POLICY_DIS","label":"Disadvantages of geographical immobility policies","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"OCC_POLICY","label":"Policies to tackle occupational immobility","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"OCC_POLICY_ADV","label":"Advantages of occupational immobility policies","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"OCC_POLICY_DIS","label":"Disadvantages of occupational immobility policies","subtopic":"3.5 Labour market"},{"id":"MONOPOLY_HARM","label":"Monopoly power raises price/restricts output causing allocative inefficiency, potential X-inefficiency and lower consumer welfare","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"MERGER_DEF","label":"A merger: two or more firms combine, potentially raising concentration/market power (may also create synergies/economies of scale)","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"MONOPSONY_DEF","label":"Monopsony power: a dominant buyer can restrict the price/wage paid and quantity traded below the competitive level","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"CONTESTABILITY_DEF","label":"Contestable market: low barriers to entry/exit create a credible threat of entry that disciplines incumbents","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"REG_CAPTURE_DEF","label":"Regulatory capture: a regulator comes to act in the interests of the industry it regulates rather than the public","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"ASYM_INFO_DEF","label":"Asymmetric information between regulator and firm: the regulator knows less than the firm about its costs, achievable quality or market conditions","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"REASON_MERGER","label":"Reasons for controlling mergers: prevent harmful increases in market power while allowing efficiency-enhancing mergers","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"REASON_MONOPOLY","label":"Reasons for controlling monopolies: correct the price/output/welfare distortions caused by monopoly power","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"REASON_COMPETITION","label":"Reasons for promoting competition/contestability: reduce market power and improve outcomes for consumers","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"REASON_PROTECT","label":"Reasons for protecting suppliers/employees: counter exploitation caused by monopsony power","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"MERGER_CONTROL","label":"Government intervention to control mergers: authorities investigate and can block, allow or approve with conditions","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"PRICE_REG","label":"Price regulation: regulator sets a maximum price a monopolist can charge (e.g. RPI-X)","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"PROFIT_REG","label":"Profit regulation: regulator caps the rate of return/profit a monopolist can earn","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"QUALITY_STD","label":"Quality standards: regulator sets minimum standards of service/product quality","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"PERF_TARGET","label":"Performance targets: regulator sets specific performance targets with penalties for failure","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"SMALL_BIZ_PROMO","label":"Promotion of small business: government support (subsidies, tax breaks, reduced red tape) to aid entry","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DEREGULATION","label":"Deregulation: removal of legal barriers to entry/exit or other restrictive regulations","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"COMP_TENDER","label":"Competitive tendering: private firms bid competitively for the right to supply a government contract","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"PRIVATISATION","label":"Privatisation: transfer of ownership of state-owned assets/enterprises to the private sector","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"MONOPSONY_RESTRICT","label":"Restrictions on monopsony power: measures (e.g. minimum wage, buyer conduct rules) limiting a dominant buyer","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"NATIONALISATION","label":"Nationalisation: transfer of ownership of private assets/enterprises into state ownership","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"ADV_MERGER_PROTECT","label":"Adv: merger control prevents harmful increases in market power, protecting consumers","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"ADV_MERGER_EFFICIENCY","label":"Adv: merger control allows efficiency-enhancing mergers to proceed while blocking anti-competitive ones","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_MERGER_INFO","label":"Dis: predicting a merger's future market effects is difficult given imperfect information, risking wrong decisions","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_MERGER_COST","label":"Dis: merger investigations are costly/time-consuming and may delay beneficial mergers","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_MERGER_CAPTURE","label":"Dis: merger authorities may be subject to regulatory capture/lobbying","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"ADV_PRICE_REG_CONSUMER","label":"Adv: price regulation moves price closer to the competitive level, raising consumer surplus","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_PRICE_REG_SET","label":"Dis: lacking full cost information, regulators may set the price cap too low (under-investment) or too high","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_PRICE_REG_CAPTURE","label":"Dis: price regulators may be captured by industry, setting prices to favour the firm","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"ADV_PROFIT_REG","label":"Adv: profit regulation limits excessive monopoly profit, freeing funds for lower prices/reinvestment","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_PROFIT_REG_GOLDPLATE","label":"Dis: tying profit to costs/capital can incentivise cost inflation or over-investment ('gold-plating')","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_PROFIT_REG_CAPTURE","label":"Dis: profit regulation is vulnerable to regulatory capture, with generous allowed returns","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"ADV_QUALITY_STD","label":"Adv: quality standards prevent firms cutting quality to raise profit, protecting consumers","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_QUALITY_STD_COST","label":"Dis: monitoring/enforcing quality standards is costly, and costs may be passed to consumers","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_QUALITY_STD_INFO","label":"Dis: regulator may lack information on achievable quality, risking standards set too low if captured","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"ADV_PERF_TARGET","label":"Adv: performance targets can incentivise improved service delivery","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_PERF_TARGET_GAME","label":"Dis: firms may manipulate data or 'game' targets without genuinely improving performance","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_PERF_TARGET_ARBITRARY","label":"Dis: performance targets may be arbitrary and not reflect what matters to consumers","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"ADV_SMALLBIZ_CHOICE","label":"Adv: promoting small business entry increases competitors and consumer choice, reducing concentration","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_SMALLBIZ_SCALE","label":"Dis: small firms may lack economies of scale, facing higher costs and risk of exit","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_SMALLBIZ_COST","label":"Dis: supporting small businesses has an opportunity cost to the taxpayer","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"ADV_DEREG_ENTRY","label":"Adv: deregulation lowers entry barriers, increasing contestability and disciplining incumbents","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_DEREG_PROTECT","label":"Dis: removing regulation may remove necessary consumer/safety protections","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_DEREG_NATMONOPOLY","label":"Dis: if reasons for regulation (e.g. natural monopoly) still hold, deregulation may not create real competition","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"ADV_TENDER_COST","label":"Adv: competitive tendering drives down the cost of providing public services through competition for the market","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_TENDER_WINNERSCURSE","label":"Dis: firms may underbid to win a contract ('winner's curse') then cut quality or fail to deliver","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_TENDER_COLLUSION","label":"Dis: a small number of bidders may collude, undermining competitive pressure","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"ADV_PRIVATISATION_EFFICIENCY","label":"Adv: the profit motive under private ownership can raise efficiency versus state ownership","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"ADV_PRIVATISATION_REVENUE","label":"Adv: privatisation raises government revenue from asset sales, reducing public borrowing","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_PRIVATISATION_MONOPOLY","label":"Dis: privatisation may convert a public monopoly into a private monopoly if the market stays uncontestable","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_PRIVATISATION_QUALITY","label":"Dis: private firms may prioritise profit over service quality or under-invest","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"ADV_MONOPRESTRICT_WAGE","label":"Adv: restricting monopsony power raises price/wage paid and quantity traded, reducing exploitation","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_MONOPRESTRICT_UNEMPLOY","label":"Dis: a wage floor set too high, or applied where power isn't truly monopsonistic, can cause unemployment","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_MONOPRESTRICT_ENFORCE","label":"Dis: restricting monopsony power carries administrative/enforcement costs","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"ADV_NATIONAL_PUBLICINTEREST","label":"Adv: nationalisation lets government prioritise public interest, quality and jobs over profit","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_NATIONAL_XINEFFICIENCY","label":"Dis: without a profit motive, state firms may suffer X-inefficiency and need taxpayer subsidy","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"DIS_NATIONAL_POLITICAL","label":"Dis: nationalised industries may suffer political interference and short-termism","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"IMPACT_PRICE","label":"Overall impact of intervention on prices","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"IMPACT_PROFIT","label":"Overall impact of intervention on profit","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"IMPACT_EFFICIENCY","label":"Overall impact of intervention on efficiency","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"IMPACT_QUALITY","label":"Overall impact of intervention on quality","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"IMPACT_CHOICE","label":"Overall impact of intervention on consumer choice","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"GLOBAL_POLICY_CONTEXT","label":"Government (competition) intervention assessed alongside macroeconomic policies in addressing national and global economic challenges","subtopic":"3.6 Government intervention"},{"id":"GLOB_DEF","label":"Definition of globalisation (growing interdependence of world economies)","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"GLOB_CHAR_TRADE","label":"Characteristic: growth in international trade of goods/services","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"GLOB_CHAR_CAPITAL","label":"Characteristic: growth in international capital flows/FDI","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"GLOB_CHAR_LABOUR","label":"Characteristic: increased international labour migration","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"GLOB_CHAR_MNC","label":"Characteristic: growth of multinational/global companies","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"GLOB_CHAR_INTERCONNECT","label":"Characteristic: greater technological/economic interconnectedness","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"GLOB_FAC_TRANSPORT","label":"Factor: falling transport costs/containerisation","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"GLOB_FAC_TECH","label":"Factor: ICT/internet advances reducing communication costs","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"GLOB_FAC_LIBERALISATION","label":"Factor: trade and financial market liberalisation/deregulation","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"GLOB_FAC_MNCGROWTH","label":"Factor: growth of MNCs seeking new markets/lower costs","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"GLOB_FAC_EMERGING","label":"Factor: opening up of emerging economies (e.g. China, India)","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"GLOB_IMP_COUNTRIES","label":"Impact of globalisation on individual countries","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"GLOB_IMP_GOV","label":"Impact of globalisation on governments","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"GLOB_IMP_PRODUCERS","label":"Impact of globalisation on producers","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"GLOB_IMP_CONSUMERS","label":"Impact of globalisation on consumers","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"GLOB_IMP_WORKERS","label":"Impact of globalisation on workers","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"GLOB_IMP_ENV","label":"Impact of globalisation on the environment","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"GLOB_IMP_MNC","label":"Impact of global companies specifically (tax avoidance, market power)","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"ABS_ADV","label":"Absolute advantage: definition","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMP_ADV","label":"Comparative advantage: definition (opportunity cost basis)","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"PPF_CONSTRUCT","label":"Construct PPF diagrams for two countries/two goods","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMP_ADV_NUMERICAL","label":"Numerical calculation of comparative advantage/gains from trade","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMP_ADV_DIAG","label":"Diagrammatic representation of comparative advantage using PPFs","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMP_ADV_ASSUMPTIONS","label":"Assumptions underlying the theory of comparative advantage","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMP_ADV_LIMITATIONS","label":"Limitations of the theory of comparative advantage","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"SPEC_ADV_EFFICIENCY","label":"Advantage: greater efficiency/output from specialisation","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"SPEC_ADV_CONSUMERCHOICE","label":"Advantage: lower prices and greater consumer choice from trade","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"SPEC_ADV_ECONOMIES","label":"Advantage: economies of scale from larger export markets","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"SPEC_ADV_COMPETITION","label":"Advantage: increased competition driving innovation","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"SPEC_ADV_GROWTH","label":"Advantage: higher economic growth/living standards from trade","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"SPEC_DIS_STRUCTURAL","label":"Disadvantage: structural unemployment from import competition","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"SPEC_DIS_OVERDEPEND","label":"Disadvantage: over-dependence/vulnerability to external shocks","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"SPEC_DIS_INFANT","label":"Disadvantage: damage to domestic/infant industries","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"SPEC_DIS_INEQUALITY","label":"Disadvantage: unevenly shared gains/increased inequality","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"SPEC_DIS_ENV","label":"Disadvantage: environmental costs of increased trade","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"PATTERN_CA","label":"Comparative advantage as a determinant of trade patterns","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"PATTERN_EMERGING","label":"Impact of emerging economies on trade patterns","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"PATTERN_BLOCS","label":"Growth of trading blocs/bilateral agreements shaping trade patterns","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"PATTERN_EXRATE","label":"Changes in relative exchange rates shaping trade patterns","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TOT_DEF","label":"Terms of trade: definition and calculation (export price index/import price index)","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TOT_FAC_EXRATE","label":"Factor: exchange rate changes affecting terms of trade","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TOT_FAC_INFLATION","label":"Factor: relative inflation/productivity affecting terms of trade","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TOT_FAC_DEMAND","label":"Factor: changes in demand for exports/imports affecting terms of trade","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TOT_FAC_PRIMARY","label":"Factor: reliance on primary products/commodity price volatility","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TOT_IMP_CURRENTACC","label":"Impact of terms of trade changes on the current account","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TOT_IMP_PURCHASING","label":"Impact of terms of trade changes on purchasing power/living standards","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TOT_IMP_GROWTH","label":"Impact of terms of trade changes on export volumes/growth","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TB_FTA","label":"Free trade area: definition","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TB_CU","label":"Customs union: definition","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TB_CM","label":"Common market: definition","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TB_MU","label":"Monetary union: definition","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TB_MU_CONDITIONS","label":"Conditions necessary for monetary union success (Eurozone)","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TB_BEN_TRADECREATION","label":"Benefit: trade creation","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TB_BEN_ECONOMIES","label":"Benefit: economies of scale from larger markets","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TB_BEN_COMPETITION","label":"Benefit: increased competition/efficiency","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TB_BEN_FDI","label":"Benefit: increased FDI inflows","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TB_BEN_POLITICAL","label":"Benefit: political/strategic cooperation","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TB_COST_TRADEDIVERSION","label":"Cost: trade diversion","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TB_COST_SOVEREIGNTY","label":"Cost: loss of economic sovereignty/policy independence","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TB_COST_UNEVEN","label":"Cost: uneven distribution of benefits between members","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TB_COST_ADJUSTMENT","label":"Cost: adjustment costs for uncompetitive domestic industries","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"WTO_ROLE","label":"Role of the WTO in trade liberalisation","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TB_WTO_CONFLICT","label":"Possible conflicts between regional trade agreements and the WTO","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"PROT_REASON_INFANT","label":"Reason for protectionism: protect infant industries","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"PROT_REASON_JOBS","label":"Reason for protectionism: protect domestic employment","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"PROT_REASON_DUMPING","label":"Reason for protectionism: prevent dumping/unfair competition","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"PROT_REASON_REVENUE","label":"Reason for protectionism: raise government revenue","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"PROT_REASON_BOP","label":"Reason for protectionism: correct a balance of payments deficit","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"PROT_REASON_STRATEGIC","label":"Reason for protectionism: protect strategic industries/national security","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"PROT_REASON_RETALIATION","label":"Reason for protectionism: retaliation against other countries' barriers","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TARIFF_DEF","label":"Tariff: definition","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"QUOTA_DEF","label":"Quota: definition","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"SUBSIDY_DEF","label":"Subsidy to domestic producers: definition","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"NTB_DEF","label":"Non-tariff barriers: definition","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"SD_DIAGRAM","label":"Construct supply and demand diagram (world price vs domestic price)","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"TARIFF_DIAGRAM_ANALYSIS","label":"Apply tariff to S&D diagram: read price, quantity, surplus, revenue, welfare loss","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"PROT_IMP_CONSUMERS","label":"Impact of protectionism on consumers","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"PROT_IMP_PRODUCERS","label":"Impact of protectionism on domestic producers","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"PROT_IMP_FOREIGN_PRODUCERS","label":"Impact of protectionism on foreign producers","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"PROT_IMP_GOV","label":"Impact of protectionism on government (revenue, trade relations)","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"PROT_IMP_LIVING","label":"Impact of protectionism on living standards","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"PROT_IMP_EQUALITY","label":"Impact of protectionism on equality","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"BOP_CURRENT","label":"Current account: definition (trade, income, transfers)","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"BOP_CAPFIN","label":"Capital and financial accounts: definition","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"BOP_CAUSE_COMPETITIVENESS","label":"Cause of current account imbalance: lack of price/non-price competitiveness","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"BOP_CAUSE_EXRATE","label":"Cause of current account imbalance: exchange rate appreciation/overvaluation","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"BOP_CAUSE_GROWTH","label":"Cause of current account imbalance: strong domestic growth raising import demand","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"BOP_CAUSE_STRUCTURAL","label":"Cause of current account imbalance: structural factors (deindustrialisation, import reliance)","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"BOP_MEASURE_EXRATE","label":"Measure to reduce imbalance: depreciation/devaluation","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"BOP_MEASURE_SUPPLYSIDE","label":"Measure to reduce imbalance: supply-side policies to boost competitiveness","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"BOP_MEASURE_PROTECTIONISM","label":"Measure to reduce imbalance: protectionist policies","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"BOP_MEASURE_DEFLATION","label":"Measure to reduce imbalance: deflationary fiscal/monetary policy","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"BOP_GLOBAL_IMBALANCE","label":"Significance of global trade imbalances","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"EXR_FLOATING","label":"Floating exchange rate system: definition","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"EXR_FIXED","label":"Fixed exchange rate system: definition","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"EXR_MANAGED","label":"Managed exchange rate system: definition","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"EXR_REVAL_APPREC","label":"Distinction between revaluation (fixed) and appreciation (floating)","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"EXR_DEVAL_DEPREC","label":"Distinction between devaluation (fixed) and depreciation (floating)","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"EXR_FAC_INTEREST","label":"Factor influencing floating rates: interest rate differentials/hot money","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"EXR_FAC_INFLATION","label":"Factor influencing floating rates: relative inflation rates","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"EXR_FAC_SPECULATION","label":"Factor influencing floating rates: speculation","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"EXR_FAC_CURRENTACC","label":"Factor influencing floating rates: current account/trade flows","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"EXR_FAC_FDI","label":"Factor influencing floating rates: FDI/investment flows","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"EXR_INTERVENE_FX","label":"Government intervention: buying/selling foreign currency reserves","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"EXR_INTERVENE_INTEREST","label":"Government intervention: using interest rates to influence exchange rate","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"EXR_COMPETITIVE_DEVAL","label":"Competitive devaluation/depreciation and its consequences","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"MARSHALL_LERNER","label":"Marshall-Lerner condition","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"J_CURVE","label":"J curve effect","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"EXR_IMP_CURRENTACC","label":"Impact of exchange rate changes on the current account","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"EXR_IMP_GROWTH_EMP","label":"Impact of exchange rate changes on growth/employment","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"EXR_IMP_INFLATION","label":"Impact of exchange rate changes on inflation","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"EXR_IMP_FDI","label":"Impact of exchange rate changes on FDI flows","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMPET_ULC","label":"Measure: relative unit labour costs","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMPET_REP","label":"Measure: relative export prices","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMPET_FAC_PRODUCTIVITY","label":"Factor: productivity/labour costs affecting competitiveness","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMPET_FAC_EXRATE","label":"Factor: exchange rate affecting competitiveness","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMPET_FAC_INFLATION","label":"Factor: relative inflation rates affecting competitiveness","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMPET_FAC_NONPRICE","label":"Factor: non-price competitiveness (quality, design, branding)","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMPET_FAC_GOVT","label":"Factor: government policy/infrastructure affecting competitiveness","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMPET_BEN_GROWTH","label":"Benefit of competitiveness: higher exports/economic growth","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMPET_BEN_BOP","label":"Benefit of competitiveness: improved current account balance","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMPET_BEN_FDI","label":"Benefit of competitiveness: increased FDI inflows","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMPET_BEN_EMPLOYMENT","label":"Benefit of competitiveness: higher employment","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMPET_PROB_DEFICIT","label":"Problem of uncompetitiveness: worsening current account deficit","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMPET_PROB_UNEMPLOYMENT","label":"Problem of uncompetitiveness: structural unemployment/deindustrialisation","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMPET_PROB_GROWTH","label":"Problem of uncompetitiveness: lower economic growth","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"COMPET_PROB_DEBT","label":"Problem of uncompetitiveness: reliance on capital inflows/debt financing","subtopic":"4.1 International economics"},{"id":"ABS_POV_DEF","label":"Definition of absolute poverty","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"REL_POV_DEF","label":"Definition of relative poverty","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"POV_DISTINCTION","label":"Distinction between absolute and relative poverty","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"ABS_POV_MEASURES","label":"Measures of absolute poverty (e.g. $-a-day threshold, basic needs basket)","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"REL_POV_MEASURES","label":"Measures of relative poverty (e.g. % below 60% of median income)","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"AP_GROWTH","label":"Economic growth raising incomes reduces absolute poverty","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"AP_UNEMPLOYMENT","label":"Rising unemployment/job losses increase absolute poverty","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"AP_INFLATION","label":"Inflation in essential goods (food, energy) increases absolute poverty","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"AP_CONFLICT","label":"Conflict, natural disasters and disease reduce output and increase absolute poverty","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"AP_POLICY","label":"Government welfare/safety-net policy reduces absolute poverty","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"RP_INCOME_DIST","label":"Changes in the distribution of income affect relative poverty","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"RP_TAX_BENEFIT","label":"Tax and benefit system (redistribution) affects relative poverty","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"RP_WAGE_GAP","label":"Skill-biased technological change/globalisation widens wage gaps, increasing relative poverty","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"RP_UNEMPLOYMENT","label":"Unemployment affects relative poverty relative to median income","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"RP_DEMOGRAPHIC","label":"Demographic change (ageing, household composition) affects relative poverty","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"WEALTH_DEF","label":"Definition of wealth (stock of assets owned)","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"INCOME_DEF","label":"Definition of income (flow of earnings over time)","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"WEALTH_INCOME_DISTINCTION","label":"Distinction between wealth inequality and income inequality","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"LORENZ_CURVE","label":"Construction of the Lorenz curve (cumulative % population vs cumulative % income/wealth, line of equality)","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"LORENZ_READING","label":"Reading/interpreting the Lorenz curve (distance from line of equality, shifts over time)","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"GINI_COEFFICIENT","label":"Gini coefficient: definition and calculation from the Lorenz curve","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"WITHIN_EDUCATION","label":"Differences in education and skills causing income inequality","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"WITHIN_LABOUR_MARKET","label":"Labour market factors (bargaining power, minimum wage, trade unions) causing income inequality","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"WITHIN_DISCRIMINATION","label":"Discrimination (gender, race, age) causing income inequality","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"WITHIN_INHERITANCE","label":"Inheritance and wealth transfers causing wealth inequality","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"WITHIN_CAPITAL_OWNERSHIP","label":"Unequal ownership of capital/assets generating unequal returns causing wealth inequality","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"WITHIN_TAX_BENEFIT","label":"Differences in tax and benefit systems affecting income/wealth inequality","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"WITHIN_TECH_GLOBAL","label":"Technological change and globalisation causing wage polarisation","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"BETWEEN_GROWTH","label":"Differing rates of economic growth between countries causing inequality between countries","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"BETWEEN_RESOURCES","label":"Differences in factor endowments/natural resources between countries","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"BETWEEN_TRADE","label":"Differences in trade patterns and terms of trade between countries","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"BETWEEN_INSTITUTIONS","label":"Differences in institutions, governance and political stability between countries","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"KUZNETS_CURVE","label":"Kuznets curve model: hypothesised inverted-U relationship between development and inequality","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"KUZNETS_RISE","label":"Early-development mechanism: industrialisation and rural-urban migration increase inequality","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"KUZNETS_FALL","label":"Later-development mechanism: redistribution, education and welfare state reduce inequality","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"DEV_EVAL","label":"Evaluation of the Kuznets hypothesis (mixed evidence, not universal pattern)","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"CAPITALISM_DEF","label":"Definition of capitalism (private ownership, profit motive, free markets)","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"CAPITALISM_MECHANISM","label":"Mechanism: returns to capital growing faster than growth/wages concentrates wealth","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"CAPITALISM_INCENTIVE_CASE","label":"Reasons capitalism may reduce poverty: incentives drive innovation, growth and job creation","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"CAPITALISM_INEQUALITY_EVAL","label":"Evaluation of the significance of capitalism for inequality","subtopic":"4.2 Poverty and inequality"},{"id":"HDI_EDU","label":"Education dimension of HDI (mean & expected years of schooling)","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"HDI_HEALTH","label":"Health dimension of HDI (life expectancy at birth)","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"HDI_LIVING","label":"Living standards dimension of HDI (GNI per capita, PPP)","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"HDI_COMB","label":"How the three HDI dimensions are combined into a single index","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"HDI_ADV1","label":"HDI captures broader wellbeing than income alone","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"HDI_ADV2","label":"HDI enables comparison of development between countries and over time","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"HDI_ADV3","label":"HDI highlights which dimension needs policy focus","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"HDI_LIM1","label":"HDI is an average that masks inequality within a country","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"HDI_LIM2","label":"HDI excludes environmental sustainability/degradation","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"HDI_LIM3","label":"HDI excludes political freedom, human rights and corruption","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"HDI_LIM4","label":"HDI relies on data that may be inaccurate or unavailable","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"OTH_IND","label":"Other indicators of development beyond HDI","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"PPD","label":"Primary product dependency","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"VCP","label":"Volatility of commodity prices","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"HDM_DEF","label":"Harrod-Domar model: growth determined by savings ratio and capital-output ratio","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"HDM_GAP","label":"Savings gap: insufficient domestic savings to finance required investment","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"FCG","label":"Foreign currency gap","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"CF","label":"Capital flight","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"DEM","label":"Demographic factors (population growth, age structure)","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"DEBT","label":"External debt burden","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"CREDIT","label":"Limited access to credit and banking","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"INFRA_F","label":"Inadequate infrastructure","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"EDUSKILL","label":"Low education/skills levels","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"PROPRIGHTS","label":"Absence of property rights","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"NONECON","label":"Impact of non-economic factors (political stability, corruption, geography, conflict)","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"TL","label":"Trade liberalisation","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"TL_ADV1","label":"Trade liberalisation exploits comparative advantage, raising export revenue","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"TL_ADV2","label":"Trade liberalisation attracts FDI and technology transfer","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"TL_DIS1","label":"Trade liberalisation exposes infant industries to competition they cannot survive","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"TL_DIS2","label":"Trade liberalisation can reinforce primary product dependency","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"FDI","label":"Promotion of Foreign Direct Investment (FDI)","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"FDI_ADV1","label":"FDI fills the savings/investment gap","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"FDI_ADV2","label":"FDI brings technology transfer and skills development","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"FDI_DIS1","label":"Profit repatriation by multinationals causes capital outflow","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"FDI_DIS2","label":"FDI may crowd out domestic firms/create enclave economies","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"RGS","label":"Removal of government subsidies","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"RGS_ADV1","label":"Removing subsidies reduces the government budget deficit/debt","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"RGS_DIS1","label":"Removing subsidies raises costs for domestic producers/consumers","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"RGS_DIS2","label":"Removing subsidies can cause short-term unemployment","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"FLEX","label":"Floating exchange rate systems","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"FLEX_ADV1","label":"Floating rates automatically correct trade imbalances","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"FLEX_DIS1","label":"Floating rates create volatility deterring trade/investment","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"FLEX_DIS2","label":"Floating rates can cause depreciation, raising import costs/inflation","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"MICRO","label":"Microfinance schemes","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"MICRO_ADV1","label":"Microfinance provides capital access for small entrepreneurs","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"MICRO_ADV2","label":"Microfinance empowers women and marginalised groups","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"MICRO_DIS1","label":"Microfinance loans can carry high interest rates causing debt cycles","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"PRIV","label":"Privatisation","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"PRIV_ADV1","label":"Privatisation raises government revenue, helping reduce debt","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"PRIV_DIS1","label":"Privatisation can create exploitative private monopolies","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"PRIV_DIS2","label":"Privatisation may lead to job losses as firms cut costs","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"HUMCAP","label":"Development of human capital","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"HUMCAP_ADV1","label":"Human capital investment raises workforce productivity and growth","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"HUMCAP_DIS1","label":"Human capital investment has long time lags and high opportunity cost","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"PROT","label":"Protectionism","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"PROT_ADV1","label":"Protectionism allows infant industries to develop","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"PROT_DIS1","label":"Protectionism can provoke retaliation, reducing export markets","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"PROT_DIS2","label":"Protectionism reduces competitive pressure, causing inefficiency/higher prices","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"MANEX","label":"Managed exchange rate systems","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"MANEX_ADV1","label":"Managed exchange rates provide stability for trade/investment planning","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"MANEX_DIS1","label":"Managing the exchange rate requires costly large reserves","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"INFRADEV","label":"Infrastructure development","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"INFRADEV_ADV1","label":"Infrastructure development reduces costs and attracts investment","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"INFRADEV_DIS1","label":"Infrastructure projects require large upfront funding, often via debt","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"JV","label":"Promoting joint ventures with global companies","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"JV_ADV1","label":"Joint ventures bring foreign capital/technology while retaining domestic ownership","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"JV_DIS1","label":"Joint ventures may create unequal bargaining power favouring the multinational partner","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"BUFFER","label":"Buffer stock schemes","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"BUFFER_ADV1","label":"Buffer stocks stabilise commodity export earnings","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"BUFFER_DIS1","label":"Buffer stocks are costly to administer/store/finance","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"BUFFER_DIS2","label":"Incorrect buffer stock pricing causes persistent surplus or shortage","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"LEWIS_S1","label":"Lewis model step 1: surplus labour in traditional agriculture","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"LEWIS_S2","label":"Lewis model step 2: labour migrates to industrial sector at constant wage","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"LEWIS_S3","label":"Lewis model step 3: industrial profits reinvested to expand capital","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"LEWIS_S4","label":"Lewis model step 4: growth continues until surplus labour exhausted (turning point)","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"LEWIS_ADV","label":"Lewis model explains historical patterns of industrialisation","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"LEWIS_DIS1","label":"Lewis model assumes the industrial sector can absorb rural migrants into jobs","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"LEWIS_DIS2","label":"Lewis model assumes industrial profits are reinvested productively","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"TOURISM","label":"Development of tourism","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"TOURISM_ADV1","label":"Tourism diversifies the economy away from primary product dependency","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"TOURISM_ADV2","label":"Tourism earns foreign currency, easing the foreign currency gap","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"TOURISM_DIS1","label":"Tourism revenue leaks abroad via foreign-owned operators","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"TOURISM_DIS2","label":"Tourism creates vulnerability to external shocks","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"PRIMARY","label":"Development of primary industries","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"PRIMARY_ADV1","label":"Developing primary industries exploits existing comparative advantage","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"PRIMARY_DIS1","label":"Focusing on primary industries reinforces dependency and price volatility exposure","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"FAIRTRADE","label":"Fairtrade schemes","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"FAIRTRADE_ADV1","label":"Fairtrade guarantees a minimum price, protecting producers from volatility","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"FAIRTRADE_DIS1","label":"Fairtrade only benefits participating producers, covering a small output share","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"AID","label":"Aid","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"AID_ADV1","label":"Aid fills the savings/investment gap","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"AID_ADV2","label":"Aid funds essential infrastructure, education and healthcare","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"AID_DIS1","label":"Aid can create dependency and reduce incentives for reform","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"AID_DIS2","label":"Aid may be diverted through corruption","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"DEBTRELIEF","label":"Debt relief","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"DEBTRELIEF_ADV1","label":"Debt relief frees government revenue for development spending","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"DEBTRELIEF_DIS1","label":"Debt relief may create moral hazard encouraging future irresponsible borrowing","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"id":"WB","label":"World Bank","subtopic":"4.3 Emerging and developing economies"},{"i